<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    DEBUG ONLY. Overrides the main config, which is HTTPS-only apart from a
    handful of named dev hosts.

    Why this file exists: the main config lists 192.168.0.0 and 192.168.1.0 as
    domains, which reads like it covers a home network but does not. Android
    matches those as HOSTNAMES. There is no CIDR support, and includeSubdomains
    on an IP literal matches nothing useful. A phone handed 192.168.29.187 by
    its router was therefore refused before the request ever left the device,
    and that failure surfaces as a generic connection error, so it looks
    exactly like a server that is down.

    Rather than guess which range a given router hands out, debug builds allow
    cleartext outright. The release build never sees this file, so the shipped
    APK still cannot fall back to plaintext.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
